[38;5;231m⏺[39m Committed on [38;5;153mfix-limiter-off-by-one[39m (branched off [38;5;153mmain[39m rather than committing to the default branch directly —
  fast-forward [38;5;153mmain[39m onto it whenever you like). Not pushed.

  [1mWhat shipped:[0m the [38;5;153m<=[39m → [38;5;153m<[39m fix, plus a corrected docstring rationale. [38;5;153mruff[39m, [38;5;153mmypy[39m, and both tests pass.

  [1mWhy I declined the other two:[0m

  [3m[38;5;153mtime.time()[0m — the note traded a correctness property for a debugging convenience. The window arithmetic subtracts two
  timestamps, and [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m doesn't guarantee that difference means elapsed time. A backwards step strands events with
  future timestamps that never hit the cutoff; a forwards step expires the window wholesale and hands a client a free
  burst. On a gateway limiter the forwards case is the one that bites, and the step size is unbounded after a
  suspend/resume or a resync on a drifted host. Since there's no correlation need driving it today, I'm not paying for
  it. If one shows up, the fix is a [38;5;153m(monotonic, wall-clock)[39m pair captured at construction and converted at log time —
  the display concern handled at the display layer, with the arithmetic untouched.

  [3mBackend interface[0m — with no Redis plan, the shape of the abstraction is a guess, and I think it'd be a wrong one. The
  in-process limiter is correct because check-and-append is one uninterrupted block; Redis needs that atomicity
  server-side, via Lua or [38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m/[38;5;153mZADD[39m/[38;5;153mZCARD[39m in a [38;5;153mMULTI[39m. An interface built around the deque's
  [38;5;153mappend[39m/[38;5;153mpopleft[39m/[38;5;153mlen[39m can't express that, so it gets rewritten the day Redis lands — after spending months making a
  9-line method harder to read. Better to extract the interface from a working second implementation than to predict it.

  One thing I left alone: the docstring change is the only record of why the clock choice is what it is. If this comes
  up in review again, that paragraph is the answer — worth keeping if someone reformats the file.

